.TH RFMSTAT 1 "FEBRUARY 2026" "1.0.0" "RFMstat Manual"
.SH NAME
rfmstat \- Wi-Fi broadcast passive statistics collector and auditor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rfmstat
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rfmstat
(Radio Frequency Monitor Statistics) is a high-performance passive auditor for Wi-Fi networks. 
It performs deep-packet analysis of 802.11 frames in monitor mode to diagnose link health, 
detect anomalies, and log security events. 
.PP
The tool uses an EPS-based (Events Per Second) detection logic, making analysis accurate 
regardless of the dwell time on a channel.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-iface " \fITEXT\fR"
Network interface to monitor. The interface \fBmust\fR be in monitor mode. 
If not specified, the system default interface is used.
.TP
.BR \-2 ", " \-\-2_4ghz " \fIRANGE\fR"
Range of channels to scan for 2.4 GHz (e.g., "1,6,11" or "1-13"). Default: all.
.TP
.BR \-5 ", " \-\-5ghz " \fIRANGE\fR"
Range of channels to scan for 5 GHz (e.g., "36,40,44" or "36-165"). Default: all.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-6ghz " \fIRANGE\fR"
Range of channels to scan for 6 GHz (e.g., "1,5,9"). Default: all.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fIUINT\fR"
Dwell time (timeout) per channel in milliseconds. 
\fB5000ms+\fR is recommended for deep audit. 
\fB100-500ms\fR is suitable for fast discovery. Default: 5000.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent
Silent mode. Suppresses per-channel real-time output and shows only final audit reports.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.SH DIAGNOSTIC TAGS
These indicators appear in brackets \fB[]\fR within the report:
.TP
.B DATA_LOSS_OR_PHY_MISMATCH
High \fIack_ratio\fR (>1.5). You see ACKs but miss Data frames. Usually occurs when the 
Wi-Fi adapter cannot decode high-speed modulations (e.g., Wi-Fi 6 traffic on a Wi-Fi 4 card).
.TP
.B HIGH_PACKET_LOSS
Low \fIack_ratio\fR (<0.1). High data volume but almost no ACKs. Indicates extreme 
interference or a client being out of range.
.TP
.B ASYMMETRIC_SIGHT
\fIrts_raw\fR > 1.05. CTS responses outnumber RTS requests. You hear the Access Point 
well, but the Client poorly.
.TP
.B KICK_ATTACK
\fIdeauth_eps\fR > 1.0. High intensity of Deauthentication frames. Active attempt to 
forcefully disconnect clients.
.TP
.B BRUTEFORCE
\fIauth_eps\fR > 5.0. High intensity of Authentication frames. Possible WPA handshake 
brute-force or flood.
.SH VERDICTS (PRIORITY ORDER)
.IP "1. \fBUNDER_ATTACK\fR"
Security threat detected (Deauth/Auth flood). Network stability is at risk.
.IP "2. \fBSATURATED\fR"
Throughput exceeds 400 Mbps. Channel is fully utilized.
.IP "3. \fBINCOMPLETE_CAPTURE\fR"
High data loss (sensor mismatch). You are missing heavy data frames.
.IP "4. \fBHIGH_L2_NOISE\fR"
Over 40% of packets are unrecognized, malformed, or noise.
.IP "5. \fBSTABLE\fR"
No anomalies detected. Channel is operating normally.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Accessing raw 802.11 frames requires \fBroot\fR privileges. Failure to provide 
sufficient permissions or specifying a non-monitor interface will result in an error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP "Audit 2.4GHz channels 1, 6, and 11 with 1s timeout:"
sudo rfmstat -i wlan0 -2 1,6,11 -t 1000
.IP "Deep audit of 5GHz channel 36:"
sudo rfmstat -i wlan0 -5 36 -t 5000
.SH AUTHOR
Written by lycan-hunter. Project home: <https://github.com/lycan-hunter/rfmstat>
